# Aggregator topology (hub side).
#
#   spokes:<id> ─┬─ firehose.p<n> ─→ Remote_Job_Rewrite ─→ Topic (firehose:topic)
#                └─ sources/php ──→ Partition (php-errors:partition)
#   php-errors:tail (the hub's own error log) ─→ php-errors:partition
#
# Remote_Job_Rewrite flips aggregated k:"job" entries to k:"remote_job" so they
# dispatch on the hub via newspack_nodes/remote_job_handlers; non-job entries
# pass through. The firehose Topic is the multi-partition, KEY-routed sink.
#
# `spokes` is a Vault_Group over Vault group `spoke`: it builds one
# Remote_Source per member, `{id}` substituted for each Vault id, and follows
# the Vault on reload — a spoke is added or dropped by editing group
# membership, never by hand-editing this file. Each Remote_Source is a broker
# holding ONE connection to its spoke, which carries both of the spoke's
# streams the pairs name, `firehose.p{partition}` and `sources/php` (the
# spoke's PHP error log); each stream is read by its own Remote_Consumer,
# published as `spokes:<id>:<kind>`, with its own cursor under
# `<topology>.{id}/`. Never hand-wire a second Remote_Source for a spoke
# already in the group beside it: two readers pulling the same spoke stream
# under independent cursors double-ingest every entry.
#
# Each pair names where its stream's lines go, which is also the declaration
# of where a spoke's lines may go: a firehose record carries no TO of its own,
# and a reader refuses any line a spoke addressed rather than letting it name
# a node in this worker's graph — the refusal consumes the line, and the
# cursor advances past it.
#
# `php-errors.p0` collects every spoke's PHP error log and the hub's own,
# which `php-errors:tail` follows; each line's FROM opens with the reader or
# the Tail that took it, so the log tells its sources apart. Worker p0 is its
# only writer, so `void_warranty` lifts its 4 KB cap and a database error line
# past PIPE_BUF still lands.
#
# Every `sources/<name>` a pair or a Tail names must resolve where it is
# read, or that leg fails loud: a spoke lacking `php` refuses the
# subscription, costing the hub that spoke's whole stream, and a hub lacking
# it fails this load. The built-in `php` needs PHP's `error_log` set to an
# absolute file path; `wp nodes doctor`'s `log-sources` row checks what each
# site reads itself.
#
# The topology mounts once per worker partition. The firehose pair names
# `{partition}`, so each worker pulls its own partition of every spoke's
# firehose; `sources/php` and the hub's own log name no partition, so worker
# p0 alone reads them. On a multi-partition hub, worker p0 carries
# `firehose.p0` and `sources/php` on one connection per spoke and tails the
# hub's own log; p1 and up each carry their own firehose partition.
#
# assume_clean_shutdown and set_multi_writer are recorded ONCE, on the group,
# and replayed to every broker present now or built later, which fans each
# out to its readers.
#
# set_multi_writer belongs on every spoke: the firehose is appended by every
# request process there, and it asks the SPOKE's reader to hold a superseded
# segment for the seal grace. Without it a straggler's last line — typically
# the request's terminal `process (complete)` — is orphaned, and the request
# never finalizes on the hub.
#
# The offsetlog + deadletter roots are the group's own ARGUMENTS, each
# reader's dirs nesting under them at `<kind>`. Scope them with `<topology>`:
# an offsetlog is a reader's cursor and the reader is the FLEET, so two hubs
# pulling one spoke stream must not share one.
#
# Each broker patrons its own SSE_In + HTTP_Out and publishes a status
# snapshot under the install-scoped cache key remote:<broker>:p{partition},
# e.g. remote:spokes:<id>:p0. SSL/HTTPS policy comes from the substrate
# vault_verify_ssl / vault_require_ssl config — not a per-topology setter.
#
# A Vault id colliding with a declared node name — `config`, the slot of the
# group's own `spokes:config` — fails the load, naming the id; on reload it
# escapes after every other spoke is built.
#
# `is_hub = 1` is what makes the site a hub: `<eln:is_hub>` reads it from the
# top-level file of each active topology, so it counts when this file is
# activated on its own, and a topology including this one declares its own.

var is_hub = 1
var on_demand_idle = 0

include topic-probe

make_node Remote_Job_Rewrite remote-job-rewrite
make_node Topic     firehose:topic       <config:logs_dir>/firehose.p{partition}
make_node Partition php-errors:partition <config:logs_dir>/php-errors.p0
make_node File_Tail php-errors:tail      sources/php <config:offsets_dir>/<topology>.php-errors.tail <config:deadletter_dir>/<topology>.php-errors.tail
make_node Vault_Group spokes Remote_Source spoke <config:offsets_dir>/<topology>.{id} <config:deadletter_dir>/<topology>.{id} firehose.p{partition}:remote-job-rewrite sources/php:php-errors:partition

command_node spokes:config assume_clean_shutdown true
command_node spokes:config set_multi_writer true
command_node php-errors:partition:config void_warranty
connect_node php-errors:tail php-errors:partition
connect_node remote-job-rewrite firehose:topic

secure
